class:: WsGendy
summary:: Dynamic stochastic synthesis bounded by zero crossings
related:: Classes/WsGen, Classes/WsMap, Classes/Gendy1
categories:: UGens>Generators>Stochastic

description::
A waveset version of Xenakis' dynamic stochastic synthesis. Each half-cycle is a curve through code::knum:: breakpoints between two zero crossings. Once per waveset every breakpoint takes a random step, and the waveset length takes a random step in octaves. Steps fold back at their limits, so the shape drifts from one waveset to the next while every waveset keeps exactly two zero crossings.

The positive and the negative half-cycle have their own breakpoints. All inputs are read once per waveset, so demand-rate inputs advance one value per waveset.

classmethods::

method:: ar

argument:: minFreq
Lowest waveset frequency in Hz.

argument:: maxFreq
Highest waveset frequency in Hz.

argument:: freqStep
Largest random step of the waveset frequency, in octaves.

argument:: ampStep
Largest random step of each breakpoint, as a fraction of the peak.

argument:: knum
Number of breakpoints in each half-cycle, 1 to 16.

argument:: amp
Peak amplitude.

argument:: skew
Position of the middle zero crossing, 0.01 to 0.99.

argument:: interp
Interpolation between breakpoints, 0 linear to 1 cosine. Cosine is smoother and aliases less.

argument:: balance
Scales down the larger half-cycle so both halves enclose the same area, which removes the DC offset that skew and uneven halves produce, 0 (off) to 1 (equal area). Zero crossings and the peak stay where they are; the level drops as the halves become more uneven.

argument:: mul

argument:: add

examples::

code::
{ WsGendy.ar(40, 120, 0.05, 0.1, 6, 0.5, Dbrown(0.1, 0.9, 0.05), 0, MouseY.kr(0, 1)) ! 2 }.play;
::

code::
{ WsGendy.ar(80, 800, 0.2, 0.1) * 0.5 ! 2 }.play;
::

code::
{ WsGendy.ar(200, 210, 0.01, MouseX.kr(0, 0.5)) * 0.5 ! 2 }.play;
::

code::
{ WsGendy.ar(MouseX.kr(20, 2000, 1), MouseX.kr(20, 2000, 1) * 1.1, MouseY.kr(0, 1), 0.05) * 0.5 ! 2 }.play;
::

code::
{ WsGendy.ar(100, 100, 0, 0.02, MouseX.kr(1, 16)) * 0.5 ! 2 }.play;
::

code::
{ WsGendy.ar(150, 150, 0, 0.1, 12, 0.5, 0.5, MouseX.kr(0, 1)) ! 2 }.play;
::

code::
{ WsGendy.ar(60, 6000, 1, 0.3, 4, 0.4, Dwhite(0.05, 0.95)) ! 2 }.play;
::

code::
{ WsGendy.ar(30, 90, 0.05, 0.02, 16, 0.5, MouseX.kr(0.05, 0.95), 1) ! 2 }.play;
::

code::
{ WsGendy.ar(Dseq([55, 110, 220, 440], inf), Dseq([60, 120, 240, 480], inf), 0.1, 0.05, 6, 0.4) ! 2 }.play;
::

code::
(
{
	var step = Dstutter(Dwhite(20, 300), Dseq([0, 0.01, 0.05, 0.3], inf));
	WsGendy.ar(100, 900, step, step, 8, 0.4, 0.5, 1) ! 2
}.play;
)
::

code::
(
{
	var knum = Dstutter(Dwhite(5, 80), Dwhite(1, 16));
	WsGendy.ar(70, 140, 0.02, 0.08, knum, 0.4, Dbrown(0.2, 0.8, 0.02)) ! 2
}.play;
)
::

code::
(
{
	Splay.ar(Array.fill(6, { |i|
		WsGendy.ar(40 * (i + 1), 44 * (i + 1), 0.01, 0.04, 3 + i, 0.35, 0.5, 1)
	}))
}.play;
)
::

code::
(
{
	Splay.ar(Array.fill(8, {
		var f = exprand(50, 400);
		WsGendy.ar(f, f * 1.02, 0.005, LFNoise1.kr(0.2).range(0, 0.15), rrand(2, 12), 0.25, 0.5, 1)
	}))
}.play;
)
::

code::
(
{
	var src = WsGendy.ar(20, 200, 0.4, 0.2, 5, 0.6, LFNoise2.kr(0.5).range(0.1, 0.9));
	LeakDC.ar(RLPF.ar(src, LFNoise1.kr(0.4).exprange(100, 4000), 0.2)) * 0.35 ! 2
}.play;
)
::

code::
(
{
	var trig = Dust.kr(3);
	var amp = Decay2.kr(trig, 0.005, TRand.kr(0.05, 0.6, trig));
	var f = TExpRand.kr(40, 1500, trig);
	WsGendy.ar(f, f * 1.5, 0.3, 0.2, TIRand.kr(2, 10, trig), amp * 0.5) ! 2
}.play;
)
::
